<Reference
  current="reference/commands"
  description="Every wayfinder-cli command — the two read-only agent commands, the setup commands, the global options, and what a usage error prints."
>
  <h1>Commands</h1>

  <p>
    <code>wayfinder</code> has two surfaces. The <strong>agent surface</strong> is two read-only
    commands. Everything else is <strong>setup</strong>: a human runs it once per repo, or an agent
    drives it with flags.
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-agent-surface">The agent surface</h2>

  <h3 id="wayfinder-skill">wayfinder skill &lt;id&gt;</h3>

  <p>
    Prints one rendered skill as markdown, on stdout. The output is the entire adapted skill. The
    CLI never summarizes content.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`wayfinder skill wayfinder
wayfinder skill prototype/ui`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    A skill id is logical. It carries no <code>.md</code> and no directory name. A skill that holds
    sub-files exposes them as sub-tree ids, and its render ends with a <strong>children block</strong
    >: one row per child, each row a command plus the condition that fires it. A single-file skill
    has no children block.
  </p>

  <p>
    The render is composed, not concatenated. A dependency that every path needs is inlined. A
    dependency that only some paths need becomes a CLI pointer. Frontmatter is stripped and headings
    are demoted, and no framing prose is added.
  </p>

  <h3 id="wayfinder-skills">wayfinder skills</h3>

  <p>Lists every served id with its description and its children.</p>

  <pre><code>{`wayfinder skills
wayfinder skills --json`}</code></pre>

  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Id</th><th>Children</th><th>Origin</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>wayfinder</code></td><td>none</td><td>core</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>grilling</code></td><td>none</td><td>core</td></tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>grill-with-docs</code></td>
          <td>
            <code>domain-modeling/adr-format</code>, <code>domain-modeling/context-format</code>
          </td>
          <td>core</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>domain-modeling</code></td>
          <td>
            <code>domain-modeling/adr-format</code>, <code>domain-modeling/context-format</code>
          </td>
          <td>core</td>
        </tr>
        <tr><td><code>research</code></td><td>none</td><td>core</td></tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>prototype</code></td>
          <td><code>prototype/logic</code>, <code>prototype/ui</code></td>
          <td>core</td>
        </tr>
        <tr><td><code>to-spec</code></td><td>none</td><td>core</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>to-tickets</code></td><td>none</td><td>core</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>tracker</code></td><td>none</td><td>tracker</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    <code>tracker</code> is not a forked skill. It prints the tracker block alone, so an agent
    working <code>to-spec</code> or <code>to-tickets</code> outside a wayfinder session can still fetch
    operations prose.
  </p>

  <h3 id="bare-wayfinder">Bare wayfinder</h3>

  <p>
    Prints the agent quickstart: the two read commands and the start line, and nothing else. <code
      >wayfinder --help</code
    > prints the full setup page instead. Two audiences, two pages.
  </p>

  <h2 id="setup-commands">Setup commands</h2>

  <p>
    Every setup command runs an interactive form when a TTY is present and no flags are given. Every
    one of them accepts flags, so an agent or a script never needs the form.
  </p>

  <h3 id="wayfinder-init">wayfinder init</h3>

  <pre><code>{`wayfinder init --harness claude,agents --tracker "github cli"`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    Writes exactly one stub skill per selected harness. Install targets are ids: <code>claude</code>
    writes to <code>.claude/skills/</code>, <code>agents</code> writes to <code>.agents/skills/</code
    >. Run it with no flags in a terminal and it offers a multi-select with detected targets marked.
  </p>

  <p>
    The stub is agent-optimized. It holds a hardened start line — <em
      >"Your next action MUST be to run <code>wayfinder skill wayfinder</code>"</em
    > — plus a generated command map of the served skills. It holds no tracker or registration talk,
    because that is config-audience material.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>init</code> is idempotent and it reports a diff. It is the first run and it is the repair
    path. There is one stub per harness, never one per served skill, so a skill you keep installed
    yourself is never overwritten.
  </p>

  <h3 id="wayfinder-tracker">wayfinder tracker</h3>

  <pre><code>{`wayfinder tracker show
wayfinder tracker set "github cli"
wayfinder tracker set "acme tracker" --doc ./docs/trackers/acme.md
wayfinder tracker set "linear mcp" --user`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    The tracker value is freeform prose. Six values ship with operations docs: <code>github cli</code
    >, <code>github mcp</code>, <code>gitlab cli</code>, <code>jira mcp</code>, <code>linear mcp</code
    >, <code>local</code>. Any other value is a custom value, and the render names it and leaves
    operations to the agent's own tools.
  </p>

  <p>
    <code>--doc</code> attaches your own operations prose to any value, known or not. It stores the <strong
      >path</strong
    >, not a copy, so the whole team reads the same file from the repo. An attached doc replaces a
    shipped doc; the two never merge.
  </p>

  <h3 id="wayfinder-ticket-skill">wayfinder ticket-skill</h3>

  <pre><code>{`wayfinder ticket-skill add pre-mortem --when "a decision is risky and hard to reverse"
wayfinder ticket-skill list
wayfinder ticket-skill edit pre-mortem --when "..."
wayfinder ticket-skill remove pre-mortem --scope project`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    A ticket skill is a harness skill that charting assigns to each ticket where it is relevant. The
    schema is three fields and no more: <strong>name</strong>, a required <strong
      >when sentence</strong
    >, and a <strong>scope</strong>. There is no source field, because the CLI points at the skill
    and never serves it.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each registration renders inside the wayfinder skill, under <strong>Ticket skills</strong>, as
    its name plus its when sentence. The charting agent reads that block and writes a ticket-carried
    pointer into each ticket the sentence covers.
  </p>

  <h3 id="wayfinder-doctor">wayfinder doctor</h3>

  <pre><code>wayfinder doctor</code></pre>

  <p>
    Checks that each registered ticket-skill name resolves to an installed harness skill, across
    user scope and project scope, for both the <code>claude</code> and the <code>agents</code>
    targets. It also reports a tracker doc path that does not resolve. A missing file is never an
    error at render time — it degrades to the no-doc block — so <code>doctor</code> is how you find it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="global-options">Global options</h2>

  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Option</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>--json</code></td>
          <td>JSON instead of the default TOON, on list and status commands</td>
        </tr>
        <tr><td><code>--version</code>, <code>-V</code></td><td>Print the version</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>--help</code>, <code>-h</code></td><td>Full setup page</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>
    List and status commands print TOON by default. Skill renders are always markdown.
  </p>

  <h2 id="usage-errors">Usage errors</h2>

  <p>
    A missing flag with no TTY exits non-zero with an error that <strong>teaches</strong>: what the
    flag means, what a good value looks like, and one example.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`error: ticket-skill add needs --when

  --when is the relevance condition for this skill. Charting reads it to
  decide which tickets carry the skill, so write it as a condition, not a
  description.

  example:
    wayfinder ticket-skill add pre-mortem \\
      --when "a decision is risky and hard to reverse"`}</code></pre>
</Reference>
